data.content:
# .NET Framework → .NET 8 Migration: BackgroundProcessor

## Critical Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET 8 — it's completely removed
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` also removed** — no equivalent exists
3. **`ThreadAbortException` is never thrown** by the runtime anymore

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _workerTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass the token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit the loop cleanly
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _cts.Cancel();

        if (_workerTask is not null)
        {
            // Wait with a timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
            var completed = _workerTask.WaitAsync(
                timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

            try
            {
                await completed;
            }
            catch (TimeoutException)
            {
                // Log warning — worker didn't stop in time.
                // There is NO forced-kill equivalent in .NET 8.
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException)
            {
                // Expected — cancellation propagated
            }
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();
        _workerTask?.Wait(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
        _cts.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **No forced termination** | .NET 8 has no `Thread.Abort` equivalent. If `GetNextItem()` blocks forever on a sync call that ignores cancellation tokens, you're stuck. You *must* make blocking calls cancellation-aware. |
| **`volatile bool` is insufficient** | A `volatile bool` can't unblock a thread waiting on I/O or `BlockingCollection.Take()`. `CancellationToken` integrates with `async` APIs, `WaitHandle`, and `BlockingCollection` natively. |
| **Sync → Async** | If `GetNextItem()` is a synchronous blocking call (e.g., `Socket.Receive`), you need to migrate it to an async overload that accepts a `CancellationToken`, or wrap it with a token-aware polling loop. |
| **Fire-and-forget `Task.Run`** | Always store and await the task. Unobserved exceptions in .NET 8 are silently swallowed (unlike .NET 4.0 which crashed the process). |
| **`IDisposable`** | `CancellationTokenSource` is disposable — failing to dispose it leaks registered callbacks. |

## If You Can't Make Blocking Calls Async

As a last resort for legacy sync code that truly can't be converted:

```csharp
// Wrapper that abandons (not aborts) an uncooperative call
private async Task<T> WithTimeout<T>(
    Func<CancellationToken, T> work,
    CancellationToken token,
    TimeSpan timeout)
{
    var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
    var completed = await Task.WhenAny(task, Task.Delay(timeout, token));
    if (completed != task)
        throw new TimeoutException("Worker did not respond to cancellation");
    return await task; // propagate exceptions
}
```

> ⚠️ This **abandons** the thread (it keeps running) rather than
> killing it. There is no safe forced-kill in .NET 8. Design all
> new code to be cancellation-cooperative.
